[2026-02-17 17:37:13] afterAgentResponse
{
  "conversation_id": "1bdc6ded-edb7-4dfe-be10-d97215f8e64a",
  "generation_id": "d901fd02-9c5c-4798-b0f0-ba664225f1fc",
  "model": "claude-4.6-opus-high-thinking",
  "text": "## Grafana Codebase Overview\n\n### Architecture at a Glance\n\nGrafana is an open-source monitoring and observability platform built as a **monorepo** with a clear split between a **Go backend** and a **TypeScript/React frontend**.\n\n| Layer | Directory | Stack |\n|---|---|---|\n| Backend | `pkg/` | Go, service-oriented, wire DI |\n| Frontend | `public/app/` | React, TypeScript, Redux |\n| Shared packages | `packages/` | npm libraries (`@grafana/ui`, `@grafana/runtime`, etc.) |\n| Plugins | `public/app/plugins/` + `pkg/plugins/` | Both sides |\n| K8s-style APIs | `pkg/apis/`, `pkg/apiserver/` | Go, moving toward resource APIs |\n| E2E tests | `e2e/`, `e2e-playwright/` | Cypress + Playwright |\n\n**Backend** (`pkg/`) is organized around ~76 service directories in `pkg/services/` (user, dashboard, alerting, etc.), a traditional REST API layer in `pkg/api/`, and a newer Kubernetes-style API layer in `pkg/apis/` and `pkg/apiserver/`.\n\n**Frontend** (`public/app/`) follows a feature-module pattern with ~54 feature directories under `public/app/features/` (dashboard, explore, alerting, datasources, bookmarks, etc.), a `core/` directory for shared framework concerns (routing, state, navigation), and a `routes/` directory for central route definitions.\n\n---\n\n### Router Architecture\n\nThe routing system is layered and currently in a transitional state between React Router **v5** and **v6**.\n\n**Installed versions** (from `package.json`):\n- `react-router@5.3.4` / `react-router-dom@5.3.4` (v5 core)\n- `react-router-dom-v5-compat@^6.26.1` (v6 compatibility bridge)\n\n**The component tree flows like this:**\n\n```\nAppWrapper (public/app/AppWrapper.tsx)\n  └── RoutesWrapper (public/app/routes/RoutesWrapper.tsx)\n        ├── Router (v5 — uses locationService.getHistory())\n        ├── LocationServiceProvider (context)\n        ├── CompatRouter (v5-compat bridge to enable v6 hooks)\n        └── AppChrome\n              └── <Routes>  (v6 API via compat)\n                    └── <Route element={...}>\n                          └── GrafanaRouteWrapper\n                                └── GrafanaRoute (auth, RBAC, error boundary)\n                                      └── route.component (lazy-loaded)\n```\n\nKey files:\n\n- **`public/app/routes/RoutesWrapper.tsx`** — The outermost router. Uses the v5 `<Router>` with `locationService.getHistory()`, then wraps everything in a `<CompatRouter>` from the v5-compat package, which unlocks v6 hooks within the v5 context.\n- **`public/app/AppWrapper.tsx`** — Renders the actual `<Routes>` and `<Route>` components using v6-style `element` props (not v5-style `component` props).\n- **`public/app/routes/routes.tsx`** — Central route registry. The `getAppRoutes()` function returns an array of `RouteDescriptor` objects (path, component, roles, chromeless, etc.). Routes use `SafeDynamicImport` for code-splitting.\n- **`public/app/core/navigation/GrafanaRoute.tsx`** — Wrapper that handles auth checks, RBAC role enforcement, and error boundaries for every route.\n\n---\n\n### The v5-to-v6 Migration\n\n#### What's Being Replaced\n\nThe legacy pattern centers on **`locationService`**, a singleton defined in `packages/grafana-runtime/src/services/LocationService.tsx`. It wraps the `history` library and provides imperative navigation:\n\n```typescript\nlocationService.push('/some/path');\nlocationService.replace('/some/path');\nlocationService.partial({ tab: 'settings' });  // update query params\nlocationService.getHistory();                    // the history object powering the v5 Router\n```\n\nThis is used pervasively — roughly **150+ files** use `.push()`, **~50** use `.partial()`, and **~24** use `.replace()`.\n\n#### What It's Being Replaced With\n\nReact Router v6's `useNavigate` hook, accessed via `react-router-dom-v5-compat`:\n\n```typescript\nimport { useNavigate } from 'react-router-dom-v5-compat';\n\nconst navigate = useNavigate();\nnavigate('/some/path');\nnavigate('/some/path', { replace: true });\n```\n\n#### Migration Strategy\n\nThe migration is **incremental and feature-flagged**:\n\n1. Each feature area gets a feature flag named `{feature}UseNavigate` (e.g., `playlistUseNavigate`, `teamsUseNavigate`), registered in `pkg/services/featuremgmt/registry.go`.\n2. Components conditionally branch:\n\n```typescript\nif (config.featureToggles.playlistUseNavigate) {\n  navigate('/playlists');\n} else {\n  locationService.push('/playlists');\n}\n```\n\n3. Once validated, the feature flag is removed and the old code path is deleted.\n\n#### Current Progress (~40%)\n\n| Status | Areas |\n|---|---|\n| **Fully migrated** | Provisioning features (uses `useNavigate` directly, no flag) |\n| **In progress / flagged** | Playlists, Teams, Service Accounts |\n| **Still on locationService** | Dashboard, Explore, Alerting, Data sources, Browse dashboards, and many others |\n| **Shared infrastructure (last to migrate)** | `useUrlParams()` hook, `useQueryParams()` hook, `interceptLinkClicks.ts`, alerting's `useURLSearchParams` |\n\nThe shared utility hooks (`useUrlParams`, `useQueryParams`) and the link-click interceptor in `public/app/core/navigation/` are the hardest pieces — they're consumed broadly and will be migrated last.\n\n#### Plugin Compatibility\n\nPlugins can import from either `react-router-dom` (v5) or `react-router-dom-v5-compat` (v6 APIs). The compat package is exposed to plugins via `public/app/features/plugins/loader/sharedDependencies.ts`, letting plugins migrate at their own pace.\n\n#### Migration Skill\n\nThere's a local skill file at `.cursor/skills/react-router-migration/SKILL.md` that documents the step-by-step workflow: add feature flag, add imports, wire up `useNavigate`, convert navigation calls, update tests, and common pitfalls to avoid. There's also a dedicated subagent type (`react-router-migration-status-explorer`) for checking migration status across the codebase.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nGrafana is a large monorepo (~130k+ files) with a Go service backend and a React feature-module frontend. The routing layer currently runs React Router v5 at the core, with a v6-compat bridge enabling incremental adoption of v6 hooks. The migration replaces the imperative `locationService` singleton with the idiomatic `useNavigate` hook, rolling out feature-by-feature behind feature flags. About 40% of navigation call sites have been migrated, with core areas like dashboards, explore, and alerting still on the legacy pattern.",
