  "model": "claude-4.6-opus-max-thinking-fast",
  "text": "Here are two approaches for a simple example GET endpoint:\n\n**Approach A (Recommended): Handler on HTTPServer** \nAdd a handler method directly to `HTTPServer` and register the route in `pkg/api/api.go`. This is the standard pattern used by existing endpoints like `/api/short-urls/:uid`. Minimal code, no new packages or wire changes needed.\n\n- New file: `pkg/api/example.go` with a `getExample` handler\n- One line added to `registerRoutes()` in `pkg/api/api.go`\n- Endpoint: `GET /api/example` returns `{ \"message\": \"Hello from Grafana!\" }`\n\n**Approach B: Separate service package**\nCreate a new `pkg/services/example/` package with its own service, then inject it into HTTPServer. This follows the full service pattern but is overkill for a hello-world endpoint -- it requires wire changes, a new interface, and `make gen-go`.\n\nI'd recommend **Approach A** -- it's the simplest path that demonstrates the core pattern (handler + route registration) without any ceremony. Does this look good to proceed with?",
