[2026-02-20 11:30:44] afterAgentResponse
{
  "conversation_id": "2675da9b-4d14-4d87-a631-99f7a178b5d9",
  "generation_id": "82faa2c5-8dd2-4c62-b7a4-9a86b4dd6a41",
  "model": "claude-4.6-opus-high-thinking",
  "text": "Here's a comprehensive overview of the Grafana codebase with deep detail on the React Router architecture.\n\n---\n\n## Codebase Overview\n\nGrafana is a large monorepo with a **Go backend** and **React/TypeScript frontend**, using Nx and Yarn for frontend build orchestration and Go modules for the backend.\n\n### Top-Level Structure\n\n| Directory | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `public/app/` | Frontend React/TS application |\n| `pkg/` | Go backend packages and services |\n| `packages/` | Shared npm packages (`grafana-ui`, `grafana-data`, `grafana-runtime`, etc.) |\n| `apps/` | Backend application modules (alerting, dashboard, folder, plugins) |\n| `conf/` | Configuration files and templates |\n| `e2e/`, `e2e-playwright/` | End-to-end tests |\n| `kinds/`, `kindsv2/` | Kubernetes resource definitions |\n| `devenv/` | Development environment setup |\n| `scripts/`, `tools/` | Build and dev tooling |\n\n### Frontend (`public/app/`)\n\n- **`features/`** — ~52 feature modules: `dashboard-scene/`, `explore/`, `alerting/`, `datasources/`, `bookmarks/`, `plugins/`, `provisioning/`, etc.\n- **`core/`** — Shared framework: components, services (`locationService`), hooks, Redux reducers/selectors, navigation logic, i18n\n- **`routes/`** — Route definitions and the router wrapper\n- **`store/`** — Redux store configuration\n- **`api/`** — API client code\n- **`extensions/`** — Extension points for plugins\n\n### Backend (`pkg/`)\n\n- **`services/`** — 30+ business logic services: `dashboards/`, `datasources/`, `auth/`, `authn/`, `authz/`, `user/`, `featuremgmt/`, etc.\n- **`api/`** — HTTP API handlers and routing\n- **`apiserver/`** — Kubernetes-style API server implementation\n- **`server/`** — HTTP server setup and middleware\n- **`tsdb/`** — Time-series database integrations\n\n---\n\n## React Router Architecture\n\n### Version & Migration Status\n\nGrafana is **mid-migration** from React Router v5 to v6:\n\n- **`react-router`**: `5.3.4`\n- **`react-router-dom`**: `5.3.4`\n- **`react-router-dom-v5-compat`**: `^6.26.1` (compatibility bridge)\n\nThe v5-compat layer allows v6 APIs (`Routes`, `Route`, `useNavigate`, `useLocation`, `useParams`) to work inside the v5 `Router`, enabling incremental migration without breaking existing code.\n\n### Router Initialization Chain\n\nThe entry flow is:\n\n1. **`AppWrapper.tsx`** — Creates `<Route>` elements using the v6 API and wraps them in `<Routes>`\n2. **`RoutesWrapper.tsx`** — Wraps everything in a v5 `<Router history={locationService.getHistory()}>` and then a `<CompatRouter>` bridge, followed by `<AppChrome>` (the shell layout)\n3. **`AppChrome.tsx`** — Provides top nav, mega menu, command palette, sidebar, and renders child routes\n\n### Route Definitions\n\nRoutes are defined centrally in `public/app/routes/routes.tsx` via the `getAppRoutes()` function, which returns a `RouteDescriptor[]`. Each descriptor has:\n\n```typescript\ninterface RouteDescriptor {\n  path: string;\n  component: GrafanaRouteComponent;  // lazy-loaded\n  roles?: () => string[];            // RBAC permissions\n  chromeless?: boolean;              // hide AppChrome\n  allowAnonymous?: boolean | ((params) => boolean);\n  pageClass?: string;\n  routeName?: string;\n  sensitive?: boolean;\n}\n```\n\nFeature-specific routes are organized in their own modules and aggregated:\n\n- `getAlertingRoutes()` — `public/app/features/alerting/routes.tsx`\n- `getProfileRoutes()` — `public/app/features/profile/routes.tsx`\n- `getDataConnectionsRoutes()` — `public/app/features/connections/routes.tsx`\n- `getProvisioningRoutes()` — `public/app/features/provisioning/`\n- `getAppPluginRoutes()` — `public/app/features/plugins/routes.tsx`\n\n### Lazy Loading\n\nAll route components use `SafeDynamicImport()` with webpack magic comments for chunk naming:\n\n```typescript\ncomponent: SafeDynamicImport(\n  () => import(/* webpackChunkName: \"DashboardPage\" */ '../features/dashboard-scene/pages/DashboardScenePage')\n),\n```\n\n### Route Guards\n\n`GrafanaRouteWrapper` in `public/app/core/navigation/GrafanaRoute.tsx` handles two layers of guarding before rendering a route:\n\n1. **Authentication** — If the route doesn't allow anonymous access and the user isn't signed in, redirect to `/login`\n2. **RBAC** — If `route.roles()` is defined, check user permissions; redirect to `/` if unauthorized\n\nEach route component is also wrapped in an `ErrorBoundary` and `Suspense` for resilience.\n\n### Feature Flag Gating\n\nRoutes can be conditionally included based on feature flags using the `&&` pattern:\n\n```typescript\nconfig.featureToggles.restoreDashboards && {\n  path: '/dashboards/recently-deleted',\n  component: SafeDynamicImport(...),\n},\n```\n\nThis means the route simply doesn't exist in the router if the flag is off.\n\n### Navigation Patterns (Two Coexisting Systems)\n\n| Pattern | Import Source | Usage Count | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `locationService.push/replace/partial` | `@grafana/runtime` | ~200+ files | Legacy (being migrated away) |\n| `useNavigate()` | `react-router-dom-v5-compat` | ~100+ files | Modern (target pattern) |\n| `useLocation()` | `react-router-dom-v5-compat` | ~50+ files | Modern |\n| `useParams()` | `react-router-dom-v5-compat` | ~40+ files | Modern |\n| `useSearchParams()` | `react-router-dom-v5-compat` | ~10+ files | Modern |\n| `<Navigate replace to=\"...\">` | `react-router-dom-v5-compat` | Various | Declarative redirects |\n\nThe `locationService` is a wrapper around a history instance — the same history instance used by the v5 `<Router>`. This is why both approaches work simultaneously: they share the same underlying history object.\n\n### Nested Routes\n\nSome features use nested `<Routes>` for sub-navigation within a feature. For example, `public/app/features/connections/Connections.tsx` defines its own nested routes relative to the parent path.\n\n### Dynamic Routing\n\nRoute parameters are used extensively:\n\n- `/d/:uid/:slug?` — Dashboard by UID\n- `/alerting/:id/edit` — Alert rule editing\n- `/a/:pluginId/*` — Plugin app pages (wildcard)\n- `/*` — Catch-all 404\n\n### Key Files to Understand the Router\n\n| File | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `public/app/routes/RoutesWrapper.tsx` | Router initialization (v5 + compat bridge) |\n| `public/app/AppWrapper.tsx` | Route rendering with v6 `<Routes>` |\n| `public/app/routes/routes.tsx` | Central route definitions |\n| `public/app/core/navigation/GrafanaRoute.tsx` | Route guards, error boundaries, suspense |\n| `public/app/core/navigation/types.ts` | `RouteDescriptor` type definition |\n| `public/app/core/components/AppChrome/AppChrome.tsx` | Layout shell wrapping all routes |\n| `packages/grafana-runtime/src/services/LocationService.tsx` | Legacy navigation service |\n\n---\n\n### Migration Direction\n\nThe codebase is moving from `locationService` (imperative, v5-style) to React Router v6 hooks (`useNavigate`, `useLocation`, `useParams`). Newer features like provisioning are fully migrated, while older features still use `locationService`. The `react-router-dom-v5-compat` bridge makes both approaches work simultaneously during this transition. A comment in `routes.tsx` notes this explicitly: *\"This will be unnecessary once/when we upgrade to React Router v6 and start using `<Routes>` instead.\"*",
