  "text": "Now I have a comprehensive picture. Here's the overview:\n\n---\n\n## Grafana Codebase Overview\n\nGrafana is a large open-source observability platform. The frontend is a React/TypeScript single-page application, and the backend is written in Go. The repository is monorepo-style with key areas:\n\n- **`public/app/`** — The main frontend application (routes, features, core components)\n- **`packages/`** — Shared libraries (`@grafana/data`, `@grafana/ui`, `@grafana/runtime`, etc.)\n- **`pkg/`** — Go backend services, APIs, and feature management\n\n---\n\n## React Router Architecture\n\nGrafana is currently in a **hybrid React Router v5/v6 state**. The architecture stacks like this:\n\n```\nRouter (v5) → LocationServiceProvider → CompatRouter (v5→v6 bridge) → Routes (v6)\n```\n\n### Key architectural files\n\n1. **`public/app/routes/RoutesWrapper.tsx`** — The root router. It uses React Router v5's `<Router>` component, powered by a `history` object from `locationService`. Inside that, a `<CompatRouter>` bridge enables v6-style hooks (`useNavigate`, `useLocation`, `useParams`) to work within the v5 tree.\n\n2. **`public/app/routes/routes.tsx`** (~622 lines) — The central route registry. `getAppRoutes()` returns an array of `RouteDescriptor` objects defining ~100+ routes. Routes use `SafeDynamicImport` for code-splitting.\n\n3. **`public/app/AppWrapper.tsx`** (~152 lines) — Maps `RouteDescriptor` objects into actual `<Route>` components and passes them into `RouterWrapper`.\n\n4. **`public/app/core/navigation/GrafanaRoute.tsx`** (~117 lines) — A per-route wrapper handling authentication, RBAC role enforcement, error boundaries, and Suspense for lazy loading. Already uses v6 hooks like `useLocation()` and `useParams()` from the compat layer.\n\n5. **`packages/grafana-runtime/src/services/LocationService.tsx`** (~197 lines) — The legacy `LocationService` singleton. A `HistoryWrapper` class wraps a `history` v4 object, exposing imperative `push()`, `replace()`, and `partial()` methods. This is what the migration is moving away from.\n\n---\n\n## The Migration: `locationService` to `useNavigate`\n\n### What's happening\n\nThe migration replaces imperative navigation calls (`locationService.push('/path')`) with React Router v6's `useNavigate()` hook. This is a necessary step toward eventually removing the v5 router and the compat layer entirely.\n\n### Current status\n\n| Metric | Count |\n|--------|-------|\n| Files using legacy `locationService` navigation | **~146** (120 production) |\n| Files fully migrated to `useNavigate` | **~16** |\n| Files partially migrated (both patterns) | **1** |\n\nBreakdown of legacy calls:\n- `locationService.push` — 80 files (62 production)\n- `locationService.replace` — 23 files (17 production)\n- `locationService.partial` — 53 files (49 production)\n\n### Migration pattern\n\nEach migration is gated behind a **per-feature feature flag** for safe rollout. The pattern is:\n\n```typescript\n// 1. Import both\nimport { useNavigate } from 'react-router-dom-v5-compat';\nimport { config, locationService } from '@grafana/runtime';\n\n// 2. Call hook at component top level\nconst navigate = useNavigate();\n\n// 3. Feature-flag the navigation\nif (config.featureToggles.yourFeatureUseNavigate) {\n  navigate('/path');\n} else {\n  locationService.push('/path');\n}\n```\n\nFeature flags are defined in `pkg/services/featuremgmt/registry.go` and generated with `make gen-feature-toggles`. The naming convention is `{feature}UseNavigate` (e.g., `playlistUseNavigate`).\n\n**Notable finding:** No `UseNavigate` feature flags currently exist in the registry, meaning no feature has completed the full flag-gated migration workflow yet. The 16 migrated files appear to have adopted `useNavigate` directly without a flag-gated transition.\n\n### Key challenge\n\nMany high-impact files are **services or classes** (like `keybindingSrv.ts`, `TimeSrv.ts`, `PlaylistSrv.ts`) where React hooks cannot be used directly. These require an adapter or dependency-injection pattern to pass in a `navigate` function, making them more complex to migrate.\n\n---\n\n## Suggested Starting Files\n\nHere are files I'd recommend exploring, ordered from easiest to most instructive:\n\n### 1. `public/app/features/playlist/PlaylistNewPage.tsx` (Easiest start)\n\nThis is a simple functional component with a single `locationService.push('/playlists')` call on line 21. It's a clean, self-contained page component — ideal for a first migration. The file you currently have open (`PlaylistCard.tsx`) doesn't use `locationService` at all, but its siblings do.\n\n### 2. `public/app/features/playlist/PlaylistEditPage.tsx`\n\nSame pattern as `PlaylistNewPage` — one `locationService.push('/playlists')` call after a form submission. Migrating both playlist pages together would be natural.\n\n### 3. `public/app/features/playlist/StartModal.tsx`\n\nSlightly more interesting: uses `locationService.push()` with `urlUtil.renderUrl()` to construct a parameterized URL. Good for learning how to handle dynamic paths with `navigate()`.\n\n### 4. `public/app/features/playlist/PlaylistSrv.ts` (Intermediate challenge)\n\nThis is a **service class** that uses `locationService.push()`, `locationService.partial()`, and `locationService.getHistory().replace()`. Since hooks can't be used in classes, this file teaches you the adapter pattern — you'd need to inject `navigate` as a dependency or refactor the service.\n\n### 5. `public/app/features/provisioning/components/Dashboards/SaveProvisionedDashboardForm.tsx` (Reference example)\n\nThis is the **only partially migrated file** in the codebase. It already imports `useNavigate` alongside `locationService`, making it a real-world example of the transition state. Study this to see how both patterns coexist.\n\n### 6. `pkg/services/featuremgmt/registry.go` (Backend side)\n\nThis is where you'd add a feature flag like `playlistUseNavigate` before starting any migration. Understanding how flags are registered here completes the full-stack picture.\n\n---\n\n## Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Start with the playlist feature** — You already have `PlaylistCard.tsx` open, and the playlist directory has simple, well-scoped components (`PlaylistNewPage`, `PlaylistEditPage`) plus a more complex service (`PlaylistSrv`) that covers both easy and intermediate migration patterns.\n\n2. **Add a feature flag** in `registry.go`, run `make gen-feature-toggles`, then implement the flag-gated navigation swap in the component.\n\n3. **Check the migration status explorer agent** — This repo has a dedicated subagent (`react-router-migration-status-explorer`) you can invoke at any time to get an up-to-date snapshot of migration progress.\n\n4. **Read the migration skill** at `.cursor/skills/react-router-migration/SKILL.md` — it contains the step-by-step workflow, testing patterns, and common errors to avoid when you're ready to start implementing.",
