  "text": "Here's a comprehensive overview of the React Router v5-to-v6 migration in the Grafana codebase.\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n### Architecture\n\nThe app currently uses a **compatibility layer** approach. In `RoutesWrapper.tsx`, the v5 `<Router>` wraps everything, with `<CompatRouter>` from `react-router-dom-v5-compat` nested inside it. This lets v5 and v6 APIs coexist during migration:\n\n```1:3:public/app/routes/RoutesWrapper.tsx\nimport { ComponentType, ReactNode, type JSX } from 'react';\nimport { Router } from 'react-router-dom';\nimport { CompatRouter } from 'react-router-dom-v5-compat';\n```\n\n### Migration Progress by the Numbers\n\n| Status | File Count |\n|---|---|\n| **Not migrated** (still using `locationService.push/replace/partial` only) | ~145 files |\n| **Migrated** (using `useNavigate` from compat layer) | ~16 files |\n| **Partially migrated** (both patterns present) | ~1 file |\n\nThe migration is roughly **10% complete** at the component level. The already-migrated files are concentrated in the **provisioning** feature area (which appears to have been systematically migrated) and a few scattered admin and alerting components.\n\n### What's Already Migrated\n\nThe provisioning feature is the most thoroughly migrated area, with `useNavigate` adopted across:\n- `ProvisioningWizard`, `ConnectionForm`, `ConfigForm`, `DeleteConnectionButton`\n- Various dashboard/folder provisioning forms and their tests\n- Wizard navigation hooks (`useWizardNavigation`, `useWizardCancellation`)\n\nA handful of other files also use `useNavigate`: `UserCreatePage`, `ConfigureIRM`, and `ConfirmVersionRestoreModal` in alerting.\n\n### Feature Flags\n\nThere are **no `*UseNavigate` feature flags** defined yet in `registry.go`. The migration skill describes a pattern of gating each migration behind a feature flag (e.g., `playlistUseNavigate`), but this hasn't been implemented for any area yet. The provisioning files appear to have migrated directly without feature-flag gating.\n\n---\n\n## What Remains (the ~60% Infrastructure Layer)\n\nBeyond individual component call sites, these larger pieces still need migration:\n\n1. **`locationService` itself** -- the central navigation abstraction wrapping the v5 `history` object. Eventually this needs to be replaced or refactored.\n2. **Route definitions** in `public/app/routes/routes.tsx` -- still using v5 `<Route>` patterns.\n3. **`RoutesWrapper.tsx`** -- still wraps everything in the v5 `<Router>` feeding `locationService.getHistory()`.\n4. **`useQueryParams` / `locationService.partial`** -- widely used for URL query parameter manipulation (~54 files use `.partial()`), and has no direct v6 equivalent.\n\n---\n\n## Recommended Next Migration Targets\n\nHere are 10 files that make excellent candidates for your next migration step, ordered from simplest to moderate complexity:\n\n### Tier 1: Quick Wins (single `locationService` call, small files)\n\n**1. `public/app/features/playlist/PlaylistNewPage.tsx`** (39 lines, 1 `push` call)\nOne `locationService.push('/playlists')` in the `onSubmit` handler. Straightforward functional component. Pairs naturally with `PlaylistEditPage`.\n\n**2. `public/app/features/playlist/PlaylistEditPage.tsx`** (52 lines, 1 `push` call)\nSame pattern -- a single `locationService.push('/playlists')` after form submission. Already imports from `react-router-dom-v5-compat` for `useParams`, so you just add `useNavigate` to the existing import.\n\n**3. `public/app/features/dashboard/components/DashNav/ShareButton.tsx`** (24 lines, 1 `partial` call)\nTiny presentational component with a single `locationService.partial()` call. In the core dashboard navigation path.\n\n**4. `public/app/core/components/AppChrome/ReturnToPrevious/ReturnToPrevious.tsx`** (55 lines, 1 `push` call)\nGlobal navigation utility with a single `locationService.push(href)` inside a `useCallback`. High user visibility, low migration risk.\n\n**5. `public/app/core/components/AppChrome/OrganizationSwitcher/OrganizationSwitcher.tsx`** (40 lines, 1 `push` call)\nHigh-traffic global chrome component. The `locationService.push` is followed by `window.location.reload()`, so the migration is low-risk since the page reloads anyway.\n\n### Tier 2: Moderate (small-medium files, 1 call, in well-understood areas)\n\n**6. `public/app/features/explore/extensions/ConfirmNavigationModal.tsx`** (44 lines, 1 `push` call)\nSmall isolated modal. The `locationService.push(path)` is in an `openInCurrentTab` handler -- clean, self-contained.\n\n**7. `public/app/features/alerting/unified/hooks/useURLSearchParams.ts`** (18 lines, 1 `partial` call)\nThe smallest file on the list. A shared hook that wraps `locationService.partial()`. Already imports from `react-router-dom-v5-compat` for `useLocation`. Migrating this has a multiplier effect since other alerting components consume this hook.\n\n**8. `public/app/features/alerting/unified/rule-editor/CloneRuleEditor.tsx`** (78 lines, 1 `replace` call)\nSingle `locationService.replace()` in an error fallback `onRemove` handler. Note: this is not a hook-based component at first glance, but `useNavigate` can be added at the top level.\n\n### Tier 3: Slightly More Complex (worth tackling for impact)\n\n**9. `public/app/features/alerting/unified/components/rule-viewer/DeleteModal.tsx`** (95 lines, 1 `replace` call)\nUses `locationService.replace('/alerting/list')` inside the `useDeleteModal` custom hook. Migrating a hook is slightly different -- you add `useNavigate` inside the hook itself.\n\n**10. `public/app/features/alerting/unified/components/extensions/ConfirmationNavigationModal.tsx`** (small, 1 `push` call)\nSimilar pattern to the Explore `ConfirmNavigationModal`. In the alerting area which is actively being worked on.\n\n---\n\n## Suggested Migration Strategy\n\n1. **Start with the playlist pair** (`PlaylistNewPage` + `PlaylistEditPage`). They're small, self-contained, and `PlaylistEditPage` already imports from the compat layer. Create a `playlistUseNavigate` feature flag and migrate both together.\n\n2. **Then tackle the global chrome components** (`ReturnToPrevious`, `OrganizationSwitcher`). These are high-visibility but trivially simple migrations.\n\n3. **Then the alerting `useURLSearchParams` hook** -- this is a force-multiplier since migrating this shared hook means all its consumers benefit.\n\n4. **Consider whether you want feature flags for each migration** or whether you want to follow the provisioning team's approach of migrating directly. The skill describes the feature-flag pattern, but no flags exist yet in practice.\n\n### Key Risk: `locationService.partial()`\n\nThe 54 files using `locationService.partial()` are the trickiest to migrate. React Router v6 doesn't have a direct equivalent for partial URL search param updates. You'll likely need a `useSearchParams`-based helper or keep `locationService.partial()` as a wrapper. The `useURLSearchParams` hook in alerting (file #7 above) is a good test case for figuring out this pattern.",
