  "text": "\nHere’s what the codebase shows:\n\n---\n\n## Does the timestamp data already exist?\n\n**No.** Per-user last-viewed timestamps are not stored or exposed anywhere.\n\n### What exists today\n\n| Source | What it stores | Timestamp? |\n|--------|----------------|------------|\n| **`impression_srv`** (localStorage) | Ordered list of dashboard UIDs (most recent first) | No – only order, no timestamps |\n| **`user_dashboard_views`** (DB table) | Schema has `user_id`, `dashboard_id`, `viewed` (datetime) | Schema exists, but no Go code reads or writes it |\n| **`dashboard_usage_sums`** | Aggregate counts (`views_last_1_days`, `views_total`, etc.) | No – counts only, not per-user or per-view timestamps |\n\n### Details\n\n1. **`impression_srv`** (`public/app/core/services/impression_srv.ts`):\n   - Stores `string[]` (UIDs) in localStorage.\n   - Order implies recency (newest first via `unshift`).\n   - No timestamps.\n\n2. **`user_dashboard_views`**:\n   - Table exists in migrations with `viewed` datetime.\n   - Grep for `user_dashboard_views` in `.go` files returns no matches.\n   - No write path and no read path in this repo; effectively unused.\n\n3. **`dashboard_usage_sums`**:\n   - Used for aggregate stats (e.g. “Views”).\n   - Not per-user and not a last-viewed timestamp.\n\n---\n\n## How to expose it through existing APIs\n\nBecause the data does not exist yet, you need both a write path and a read path.\n\n### 1. Write path (recording views)\n\n- Add backend logic to upsert into `user_dashboard_views` when a dashboard is viewed.\n- Likely place: `GetDashboard` in `pkg/api/dashboard.go` (around line 88), or equivalent in the dashboard service.\n- Use `INSERT ... ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE viewed = NOW()` (or equivalent) to keep the latest view per user/dashboard.\n\n### 2. Read path (exposing via APIs)\n\n**Option A: Extend the search API**\n\n- Unified search: `/apis/dashboard.grafana.app/v0alpha1/namespaces/{ns}/search`\n- Each hit has `field: Record<string, string | number>` for extra fields.\n- Add a `last_viewed` (or `last_viewed_at`) field in the backend search builder and include it in `hit.field`.\n- Frontend already maps `hit.field` into the DataFrame in `unified.ts` (around 391–413), so new fields would flow through automatically.\n\n**Option B: Extend the legacy search API**\n\n- Legacy search: `GET /api/search`\n- Returns `model.Hit` with `SortMeta` (int64) and `SortMetaName` (string).\n- You could add a dedicated field for last-viewed (e.g. `LastViewedAt`) on the hit model and populate it from `user_dashboard_views` in the search handler.\n\n**Option C: New endpoint**\n\n- Add something like `GET /api/dashboards/recently-viewed` that:\n  - Reads from `user_dashboard_views` for the current user.\n  - Returns dashboards with `viewed` timestamps.\n- Use this for the “Recently viewed” section instead of `impression_srv`.\n\n### 3. Search flow (for Option A)\n\n```\nBackend: search builder → includes last_viewed in document/fields\n       → ResourceSearchResponse hits\n       → each hit.field[\"last_viewed\"] = timestamp\n\nFrontend: unified.ts toDashboardResults() → hit.field spread into result\n        → queryResultToViewItem() → can map to sortMeta or new property\n        → DashboardViewItem / DashboardQueryResult\n```\n\n`DashboardQueryResult` has `[key: string]: unknown`, so extra fields from the backend can be passed through without schema changes.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n- Per-user last-viewed timestamps are not stored or exposed anywhere.\n- `user_dashboard_views` has the right schema but is unused.\n- To expose timestamps via existing APIs:\n  1. Implement writes to `user_dashboard_views` when dashboards are viewed.\n  2. Extend the search API (unified or legacy) to include `last_viewed` (or similar) in the hit payload.\n  3. Optionally add a dedicated “recently viewed” endpoint if you want a separate API for that section.",
