  "text": "\nYou can do most cosmetic categorization work inside `BookmarksPage.tsx`, with one important dependency.\n\n---\n\n## What you can do in `BookmarksPage.tsx` only\n\n- **Use existing category styles** – `NavLandingPageCard` already accepts a `category` prop (`'primary' | 'secondary' | 'success' | 'warning' | 'error'`). You can derive a category from the nav item and pass it:\n\n```tsx\n<NavLandingPageCard\n  key={item.id || item.url}\n  description={item.subTitle}\n  text={item.text}\n  url={item.url ?? ''}\n  category={deriveCategory(item)}  // e.g. from item.parentItem?.id, item.pluginId, item.url\n/>\n```\n\n- **Grouping** – Add section headers, group items by category, or change the grid layout.\n- **Layout** – Adjust `getStyles` (grid, spacing, etc.).\n\n`NavModelItem` has `id`, `parentItem`, `pluginId`, `url`, and `text`, so you can derive categories without changing other files.\n\n---\n\n## When you need to touch other files\n\n| Scenario | File to change |\n|----------|----------------|\n| New category styles (extra colors, borders, etc.) | `public/app/core/components/NavLandingPage/NavLandingPageCard.tsx` |\n| Different card layout or structure | `NavLandingPageCard.tsx` or a new card component |\n| Categorization based on user preferences or backend data | `hooks.ts`, preferences API, or similar |\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nFor cosmetic categorization using the existing five category styles and grouping/layout changes, you can stay in `BookmarksPage.tsx`. If you need new visual styles for categories, you’ll need to update `NavLandingPageCard.tsx` as well.",
