  "model": "composer-1",
  "text": "\nFor lightweight categorization, you can do most of it in `BookmarksPage.tsx`.\n\n## What you can do in `BookmarksPage.tsx` alone:\n\n1. Group bookmarks by `parentItem` — `NavModelItem` has a `parentItem` property (e.g., \"Dashboards\", \"Alerting\", \"Configuration\") that you can use to group.\n2. Add section headers — render category headers before each group.\n3. Use existing category styling — `NavLandingPageCard` already supports a `category` prop (`'primary' | 'secondary' | 'success' | 'warning' | 'error'`) for visual styling.\n\n## When you might need `NavLandingPageCard.tsx`:\n\n- Only if you need more category options beyond the 5 existing ones (`primary`, `secondary`, `success`, `warning`, `error`).\n- The component already handles category styling, so you can pass the `category` prop from `BookmarksPage.tsx`.\n\n## Example approach:\n\n```typescript\n// In BookmarksPage.tsx - group by parentItem\nconst groupedItems = validItems.reduce((acc, item) => {\n  const category = item.parentItem?.text || 'Other';\n  if (!acc[category]) acc[category] = [];\n  acc[category].push(item);\n  return acc;\n}, {} as Record<string, NavModelItem[]>);\n\n// Then render with section headers\n{Object.entries(groupedItems).map(([category, items]) => (\n  <section key={category}>\n    <h2>{category}</h2>\n    <div className={styles.grid}>\n      {items.map(item => (\n        <NavLandingPageCard\n          category={getCategoryStyle(category)} // optional styling\n          ...\n        />\n      ))}\n    </div>\n  </section>\n))}\n```\n\nBottom line: `BookmarksPage.tsx` should be sufficient for lightweight categorization. You’ll only need `NavLandingPageCard.tsx` if you want to extend the category options.",
