  "text": "Here's a comprehensive overview.\n\n---\n\n## Codebase Overview\n\nGrafana is a large-scale open-source observability and monitoring platform. The repo is a monorepo containing:\n\n- **Go backend** (`pkg/`) — HTTP APIs, services, data access, feature management, alerting engine, and Kubernetes-style resource APIs\n- **TypeScript/React frontend** (`public/app/`) — the full SPA UI, organized by feature area\n- **Shared packages** (`packages/`) — libraries like `@grafana/data`, `@grafana/ui`, `@grafana/runtime` consumed by both core and plugins\n- **Plugin ecosystem** (`public/app/plugins/`) — built-in panels, data sources, and app plugins\n\nThe frontend uses Redux for state management, `@grafana/ui` as a component library, and React Router for client-side routing.\n\n---\n\n## React Router Architecture\n\n### Current State: Hybrid v5/v6 Bridge\n\nGrafana is in a **transitional hybrid architecture** that bridges React Router v5 and v6. Here's the component hierarchy:\n\n```\nAppWrapper (Redux, Theme, KBar, Scopes providers)\n  └─ RouterWrapper (RoutesWrapper.tsx)\n       └─ <Router history={locationService.getHistory()}>    ← React Router v5\n            └─ <LocationServiceProvider>                     ← Legacy singleton\n                 └─ <CompatRouter>                           ← v5-compat bridge\n                      └─ <Routes> / <Route>                  ← v6 API via compat\n                           └─ GrafanaRouteWrapper            ← Auth + rendering\n                                └─ Page Component\n```\n\nThe key layers are:\n\n| Layer | Package | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| Outer router | `react-router-dom` v5.3.4 | Root `<Router>` consuming `history` v4 |\n| History | `history` v4 via `locationService` | Single history instance shared across both patterns |\n| Compat bridge | `react-router-dom-v5-compat` v6.26.1 | `<CompatRouter>` enables v6 hooks inside v5 tree |\n| Route rendering | v6 `<Routes>` / `<Route>` | Route matching uses v6 API |\n| Legacy navigation | `locationService` singleton | `push()`, `replace()`, `partial()` — imperative API |\n| Modern navigation | `useNavigate()` hook | React hook from v5-compat — the target pattern |\n\nBoth navigation patterns work because they share the same underlying `history` instance. This is what makes the incremental migration possible.\n\n### Key Files\n\n| File | Role |\n|---|---|\n| `public/app/routes/RoutesWrapper.tsx` | Root router setup — mounts v5 Router + CompatRouter bridge |\n| `public/app/routes/routes.tsx` | All route definitions via `getAppRoutes()` |\n| `public/app/AppWrapper.tsx` | App root, provider hierarchy, renders `<Routes>` |\n| `public/app/core/navigation/GrafanaRoute.tsx` | Route wrapper handling auth, analytics, error boundaries |\n| `packages/grafana-runtime/src/services/LocationService.tsx` | The `locationService` singleton (legacy) |\n| `public/app/core/navigation/patch/interceptLinkClicks.ts` | Global `<a>` click interceptor that routes through `locationService` |\n| `pkg/services/featuremgmt/registry.go` | Feature flag definitions (backend) |\n\n### Target End State\n\nThe migration goal is a clean React Router v6 setup:\n\n```\n<BrowserRouter>  (React Router v6 native)\n  └─ <Routes>\n       └─ <Route element={...} />\n  Navigation: useNavigate() only\n  Location:   useLocation() only\n  Params:     useParams() only\n```\n\nNo more `locationService` singleton, no v5 Router, no CompatRouter bridge, no global link click interceptor.\n\n---\n\n## Migration Status\n\n### Overall Progress\n\n| Metric | Count |\n|---|---|\n| Files using legacy `locationService` only | **122** |\n| Files fully migrated to `useNavigate` | **16** |\n| Files partially migrated (both patterns) | **1** |\n| **Total files with navigation calls** | **139** |\n| **Migration completion** | **~10%** |\n\n### By Feature Area\n\n| Area | Legacy Files | Migrated | Status |\n|---|---:|---:|---|\n| Dashboards (dashboard + dashboard-scene + browse-dashboards) | 67 | 0 | Not started |\n| Alerting | 22 | 1 | Early |\n| **Provisioning** | **2** | **14** | **Nearly complete** |\n| Scopes | 6 | 0 | Not started |\n| Playlist | 5 | 0 | Not started |\n| Variables | 5 | 0 | Not started |\n| Explore | 4 | 0 | Not started |\n| Core infrastructure | 8 | 0 | Not started |\n\nProvisioning is the reference implementation — 14 of 16 files already use `useNavigate`.\n\n### Migration Pattern\n\nEach migration is gated behind a feature flag (`{feature}UseNavigate`) for safe rollout:\n\n```typescript\nimport { config, locationService } from '@grafana/runtime';\nimport { useNavigate } from 'react-router-dom-v5-compat';\n\nconst navigate = useNavigate();\n\nif (config.featureToggles.playlistUseNavigate) {\n  navigate('/playlists');\n} else {\n  locationService.push('/playlists');\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Recommended Starter Files for a Small-Scale Test\n\nHere are three ideal candidates, ranked by simplicity:\n\n### 1. `PlaylistNewPage.tsx` (39 lines, 1 nav call) — Best starting point\n\n```1:39:public/app/features/playlist/PlaylistNewPage.tsx\nimport { useState } from 'react';\n\nimport { NavModelItem } from '@grafana/data';\nimport { t } from '@grafana/i18n';\nimport { locationService } from '@grafana/runtime';\nimport { Page } from 'app/core/components/Page/Page';\n\nimport { Playlist, useCreatePlaylistMutation } from '../../api/clients/playlist/v0alpha1';\n\nimport { PlaylistForm } from './PlaylistForm';\nimport { getDefaultPlaylist } from './utils';\n\nexport const PlaylistNewPage = () => {\n  const [playlist] = useState<Playlist>(getDefaultPlaylist());\n  const [createPlaylist] = useCreatePlaylistMutation();\n\n  const onSubmit = async (playlist: Playlist) => {\n    await createPlaylist({\n      playlist,\n    });\n    locationService.push('/playlists');\n  };\n  // ... rest of component\n};\n```\n\n**Why it's ideal**: Only 39 lines, one `locationService.push('/playlists')` call on line 21, already a functional React component (so `useNavigate` hook works directly), no complex state or nesting.\n\n### 2. `PlaylistEditPage.tsx` (52 lines, 1 nav call) — Natural companion\n\n```1:52:public/app/features/playlist/PlaylistEditPage.tsx\n// Already imports from react-router-dom-v5-compat (useParams)\nimport { useParams } from 'react-router-dom-v5-compat';\n// ...\n  const onSubmit = async (playlist: Playlist) => {\n    replacePlaylist({ ... });\n    locationService.push('/playlists');\n  };\n```\n\n**Why it's ideal**: Nearly identical pattern to `PlaylistNewPage`, already imports from `react-router-dom-v5-compat` (for `useParams`), so adding `useNavigate` to the same import is trivial. Same single navigation call to `/playlists`.\n\n### 3. `CreateTeam.tsx` (120 lines, 1 nav call) — Slightly more complex\n\n```39:58:public/app/features/teams/CreateTeam.tsx\n  const createTeam = async (formModel: TeamDTO) => {\n    try {\n      const { data, error } = await createTeamTrigger(\n        {\n          email: formModel.email || '',\n          name: formModel.name,\n        },\n        pendingRoles\n      );\n      // ...\n      if (data && data.uid) {\n        locationService.push(`/org/teams/edit/${data.uid}`);\n      }\n    } catch (e) {\n      // ...\n    }\n  };\n```\n\n**Why it's good**: Still just one navigation call, a standalone page component, and the dynamic path (`/org/teams/edit/${data.uid}`) tests a slightly more realistic pattern than a static path.\n\n### Migration Steps for These Files\n\nFor each file, the workflow is:\n\n1. **Register feature flag** in `pkg/services/featuremgmt/registry.go` (e.g., `playlistUseNavigate`)\n2. **Run** `make gen-feature-toggles` to generate TypeScript types\n3. **Add** `useNavigate` import from `react-router-dom-v5-compat`\n4. **Add** `const navigate = useNavigate()` at component top level\n5. **Wrap** the `locationService.push()` call with a feature-flag conditional\n6. **Write tests** that cover both the feature-flag-on and feature-flag-off paths\n\nThe playlist files pair naturally together (same feature flag, same target path), making them a clean two-file migration you could do as a single PR to validate the entire workflow end-to-end.",
