  "model": "claude-4.6-opus-high-thinking",
  "text": "Here's a comprehensive overview of the Grafana codebase and its React Router migration.\n\n---\n\n## Grafana Codebase Overview\n\nGrafana is a large-scale monorepo with a **Go backend** and a **React/TypeScript frontend**, plus a suite of shared frontend packages.\n\n### Top-level structure\n\n| Directory | Role |\n|-----------|------|\n| `pkg/` | Go backend — HTTP API, services, data source backends, middleware, Kubernetes-style API server |\n| `public/app/` | React frontend — feature modules, routing, core infrastructure, Redux store |\n| `packages/` | Shared frontend packages (`grafana-ui`, `grafana-data`, `grafana-runtime`, `grafana-schema`, etc.) |\n| `apps/` | Standalone Grafana sub-applications (alerting, dashboard, playlist, provisioning, etc.) |\n| `conf/` | Default configuration |\n| `e2e/` / `e2e-playwright/` | End-to-end tests |\n| `kinds/` | CUE schema definitions for Grafana kinds |\n\n### Frontend architecture (`public/app/`)\n\nThe frontend is organized into **feature modules** under `public/app/features/`, each owning its own UI, state, and routing. Key areas include:\n\n- **Dashboards**: `dashboard/`, `dashboard-scene/`, `browse-dashboards/`, `manage-dashboards/`\n- **Alerting**: `alerting/`\n- **Data**: `datasources/`, `connections/`, `explore/`, `query/`\n- **Admin**: `admin/`, `provisioning/`, `users/`, `teams/`\n- **Other**: `playlist/`, `profile/`, `plugins/`, `variables/`, `scopes/`, `annotations/`, etc.\n\nCore infrastructure lives in `public/app/core/` (navigation, services, hooks, reducers, internationalization).\n\n### Backend architecture (`pkg/`)\n\nThe backend follows a layered service architecture:\n\n- `pkg/api/` — HTTP handlers and routing\n- `pkg/services/` — Domain services (dashboards, users, auth, alerting, etc.)\n- `pkg/registry/` — Kubernetes-style API registries\n- `pkg/tsdb/` — Built-in data source backends (Prometheus, Loki, etc.)\n- `pkg/infra/` — Infrastructure (database, logging, tracing, caching)\n\n---\n\n## React Router Architecture\n\n### Current state: transitional hybrid (v5 + v6 compat)\n\nGrafana currently runs **React Router v5 (5.3.4)** as the root router, with a **v5-compat bridge** (`react-router-dom-v5-compat` v6.26.1) layered inside, enabling v6 APIs within the v5 tree.\n\nThe router stack, defined in `public/app/routes/RoutesWrapper.tsx`:\n\n```\nRouter (v5, using history from locationService)\n  → LocationServiceProvider\n    → CompatRouter (v5-compat bridge to v6 APIs)\n      → QueriesDrawerContextProvider\n        → ExtraProviders (enterprise)\n          → ModalsContextProvider\n            → AppChrome\n              → <Routes> / <Route> (v6 API via compat layer)\n                → GrafanaRouteWrapper (auth/access control)\n                  → GrafanaRoute (error boundary + suspense)\n                    → Page Component\n```\n\n### Two coexisting navigation patterns\n\nBoth patterns work because they share the same underlying `history` instance owned by `locationService`:\n\n**Legacy (locationService singleton)**:\n```typescript\nimport { locationService } from '@grafana/runtime';\nlocationService.push('/path');\nlocationService.replace('/path');\nlocationService.partial({ query: 'value' });\n```\n\n**Modern (useNavigate hook)**:\n```typescript\nimport { useNavigate } from 'react-router-dom-v5-compat';\nconst navigate = useNavigate();\nnavigate('/path');\nnavigate('/path', { replace: true });\n```\n\n### Route definitions\n\nRoutes are centrally defined in `public/app/routes/routes.tsx` via `getAppRoutes()`, which returns a `RouteDescriptor[]`. Each descriptor specifies a path, component, roles, and rendering options. Feature areas contribute their own routes (alerting, provisioning, plugins, etc.) which are composed together.\n\n### Key routing files\n\n| File | Purpose |\n|------|---------|\n| `public/app/routes/RoutesWrapper.tsx` | Root router setup (v5 Router + CompatRouter bridge) |\n| `public/app/routes/routes.tsx` | Centralized route definitions (`getAppRoutes()`) |\n| `public/app/AppWrapper.tsx` | App root, provider hierarchy, renders `<Routes>` |\n| `public/app/core/navigation/GrafanaRoute.tsx` | Route wrapper (auth, error boundary, suspense) |\n| `public/app/core/navigation/patch/interceptLinkClicks.ts` | Global `<a>` click interceptor → `locationService.push()` |\n| `packages/grafana-runtime/src/services/LocationService.tsx` | `locationService` singleton |\n\n---\n\n## Migration from v5 to v6\n\n### Progress snapshot\n\n```\nMigration status (source files)\n\n  Legacy only (locationService)   ████████████████████  145\n  Migrated (useNavigate)          ██                     16\n  Partially migrated              ▏                       1\n\n  Total files with navigation calls: 162\n  ~10% migrated\n```\n\n**Navigation API usage across the codebase**:\n\n| API | Files |\n|-----|------:|\n| `locationService.push()` | 80 |\n| `locationService.partial()` | 54 |\n| `locationService.replace()` | 23 |\n| `useNavigate()` | 17 |\n\n### Migration by feature area\n\n| Area | Legacy files | useNavigate files | Status |\n|------|------------:|------------------:|--------|\n| Dashboards (all) | 67 | 0 | Not started |\n| Alerting | 22 | 1 | Early |\n| **Provisioning** | **2** | **14** | **Nearly complete** |\n| Scopes | 6 | 0 | Not started |\n| Playlist | 5 | 0 | Not started |\n| Variables | 5 | 0 | Not started |\n| Explore | 4 | 0 | Not started |\n| Core infrastructure | 8 | 0 | Not started |\n\n**Provisioning** is the most advanced area, with 14 of 16 navigation files already using `useNavigate`.\n\n### Migration pattern\n\nThe established approach uses **feature-flag-gated dual navigation** for safe, incremental rollout:\n\n```typescript\nimport { config, locationService } from '@grafana/runtime';\nimport { useNavigate } from 'react-router-dom-v5-compat';\n\nconst navigate = useNavigate();\n\nif (config.featureToggles.playlistUseNavigate) {\n  navigate('/playlists');\n} else {\n  locationService.push('/playlists');\n}\n```\n\nFeature flags follow the naming convention `{feature}UseNavigate` and are defined in `pkg/services/featuremgmt/registry.go`. After adding a flag, `make gen-feature-toggles` regenerates the TypeScript types.\n\n### Key challenges remaining\n\n1. **Dashboards are the largest surface** — 67 files, needs phased rollout with feature flags\n2. **`locationService.partial()` has no direct v6 equivalent** — 54 files use it for query-param-only updates; requires a utility wrapper on top of `useNavigate` + `useLocation`\n3. **Non-React code can't use hooks** — `keybindingSrv.ts`, `interceptLinkClicks.ts`, and other imperative services need architectural changes to receive a navigate function\n4. **Global link click interceptor** — `interceptLinkClicks.ts` funnels all `<a>` clicks through `locationService.push()` and must be replaced with a React Router-aware mechanism\n5. **Test files** — Many tests assert against `locationService.getLocation()` and need updating to mock `useNavigate` instead\n\n### Target end state\n\nThe goal is to remove the entire v5/compat bridge and run React Router v6 natively:\n\n```\n<BrowserRouter>  (React Router v6)\n  <Routes>\n    <Route element={...} />\n    ...\n  </Routes>\n\n  Navigation: useNavigate() only\n  Location:   useLocation() only\n  Params:     useParams() only\n```\n\nThis means removing:\n- The `locationService` singleton as a navigation mechanism\n- The v5 `<Router>` and `<CompatRouter>` bridge\n- The global link click interceptor\n\n---\n\nThe migration skill at `.cursor/skills/react-router-migration/SKILL.md` provides step-by-step instructions for contributors migrating individual components. The detailed status is tracked in `docs/react-router-migration-overview.md`.",
