fill:#fff3e0,stroke:#e65100\n    style PEP_PARAMS fill:#c8e6c9,stroke:#2e7d32\n```\n\n### Why these two files are ideal first candidates\n\n**`PlaylistNewPage.tsx`** (39 lines) and **`PlaylistEditPage.tsx`** (52 lines) are excellent migration targets because:\n\n1. **Simple, single navigation call** — Each file has exactly one `locationService.push('/playlists')` call, triggered after a form submit\n2. **Already functional components** — `useNavigate()` is a hook, so it only works in function components. Both already are.\n3. **`PlaylistEditPage` already uses v6 compat** — It imports `useParams` from `react-router-dom-v5-compat` (line 1), so adding `useNavigate` to the same import is trivial\n4. **No `partial()` usage** — Neither uses `locationService.partial()`, which has no direct v6 equivalent and would require a utility wrapper\n5. **Deterministic target route** — Both always navigate to the same path (`/playlists`), no dynamic URL construction\n\n### What the migration would look like\n\nFor `PlaylistEditPage.tsx`, for example, the change is minimal:\n\n```typescript\n// Before (line 1, 5, 26)\nimport { useParams } from 'react-router-dom-v5-compat';\nimport { locationService } from '@grafana/runtime';\n// ...\nlocationService.push('/playlists');\n\n// After\nimport { useNavigate, useParams } from 'react-router-dom-v5-compat';\nimport { config, locationService } from '@grafana/runtime';\n// ...\nconst navigate = useNavigate();\n// ...\nif (config.featureToggles.playlistUseNavigate) {\n  navigate('/playlists');\n} else {\n  locationService.push('/playlists');\n}\n```\n\n### Contrast with harder files\n\n`PlaylistSrv.ts` is a **class-based service** (not a React component), so it can't call `useNavigate()`. It also uses `locationService.getHistory().replace()`, `locationService.push()`, and `locationService.partial()` — all three legacy APIs. That file requires architectural changes (injecting a navigate function or restructuring as a React component) and would come later in the migration.",
